<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jahangir Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1628">1628</a>–<a href="/years/1652">1652</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Salqam Jahangir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jahangir Khan was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> from 1628 to 1652 and a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esim-khan">Esim Khan</a>. In 1643, with a small force armed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> and help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalangtush-bahadur">Yalangtush Bahadur</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, he is said to have checked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdeni-batur">Erdeni Batur</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-orbulaq">Battle of Orbulaq</a> in the mountains of Zhetysu. He was killed fighting the Dzungars in 1652.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nothing reliable is known of the year of Jahangir's birth. Like his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esim-khan">Esim Khan</a>, he was concerned by the threat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>, a Buddhist people who occupied the lands near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and warred constantly with the Muslim Uyghurs. The danger grew after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdeni-batur">Erdeni Batur</a>, ruler from 1635 to 1654, created a strong centralised state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> were divided by internal strife: each Chingizid claimed supremacy, and the beys fiercely resisted the sultans' attempts to strengthen the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The exact date of his proclamation as khan has not been established. According to some reports he was raised on a white rug immediately after his father's death, but his recognition as supreme khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> came only slowly, and the constant political struggle meant that he always had to prove his inherited right to rule. In 1635 he was captured in battle by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> and released after a peace treaty, on condition that he no longer trouble the Dzungar Khanate.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdeni-batur">Erdeni Batur</a> seized lands in the southern and south-eastern spurs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> in 1643, Jahangir went to meet his army with a host of 600, against a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> force of about 50,000. He chose a place near the river Orbulaq in the Jungar Alatau and fought from trenches. As A. I. Levshin described it, he placed part of his force in a gorge between two mountains behind a deep moat and a high rampart dug in advance, a fortification 2.5 to 3 km long.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The victory at Orbulaq earned him the nickname Salqam, meaning impressive or powerful, and he is often called Salqam-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangir-khan">Jangir Khan</a>. He led further campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> that allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> to rebuild a strong state, and he was given the honorific title Shah-i-Turan, Persian for King of Turan, for strengthening the khanate. Historians have described him as a leader of determination, perseverance and endurance, whose rule saved the Kazakh tribes from civil war and external threat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salqam Jangir Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1643 <b>Jahangir Khan</b>, with a small force of musketeers and help from Yalangtush Bahadur of Samarkand, is said to have checked the Dzungar ruler Erdeni Batur at the Battle of Orbulaq in the mountains of Zhetysu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Turkestan, in what is now <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. Died in the Kazakh Khanate. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.37,67.96&amp;year=1640" aria-label="Turkestan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6230 753 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jahangir Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6680 1023h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6680 1023h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.37,67.96&amp;year=1640">What was here in 1640? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
